WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.990
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:06.760
If you're travelling in the vast wilderness
of British Columbia, along the wild Alaskan

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:13.240
border, you'll come across a gigantic and
most impressive fjord, in the middle of nowhere.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:18.770
At the very tip of that fjord you'll find
a seemingly beautiful, quaint little town

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.130
called Kitsault.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.960
Hundreds of miles from anywhere, you look
over at the remote town, nestled within beautiful

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:32.570
surrounds and you observe that all the lights
are on, windows emit a warm glow of life.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:37.340
There must be people home, this seems like
quite a lively community, you would think.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:44.840
But as you walk closer you come across a large,
locked gate and a sign warning off trespassers.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:51.000
You start to realise that not all is as seems
and there is in fact something odd and rather

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.120
eerie about this estranged settlement.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.380
It's completely empty.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:03.719
Kitsault is a ghost town, its hundreds of
homes, shopping mall, sports hall and shops

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:07.119
are all completely deserted.

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:13.749
Yet the lights are still on, power still runs
to the town, homes are lit and tables are

00:01:13.749 --> 00:01:19.060
set just as if the entire town upped and left
at a moments notice.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.929
Because that is exactly what happened.

00:01:21.929 --> 00:01:27.810
Kitsault was built in 1979 to house the families
that worked at the nearby Molybdenum mine.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:33.439
Molybdenum is a metal that was in very high
demand by manufacturing industries and Kitsault

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:38.319
was a promising new start for many people,
offering a well-paid job at the nearby mine

00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:44.719
and a beautiful new community, built at a
cost of $43 million, to live amongst.

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:52.009
Kitsault was home to 1,200 residents and offered
a hospital, sports centre, shopping mall,

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:58.179
restaurants, theatre, grocery store, library
and of course local pub.

00:01:58.179 --> 00:02:05.819
Life was perfect, until one day, just 18 months
after the town had been built, the price of

00:02:05.819 --> 00:02:13.770
Molybdenum crashed and the mine was shut down,
depriving over a thousand people of a job.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:19.159
With no reason left to continue to support
the town financially the owners of Kitsault

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:21.340
evicted everyone.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:26.470
Pretty much overnight the entire town's residents
were forced to leave their lives behind and

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.650
move, never to return.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:34.819
But many residents left lights on at home
and in public buildings and the power to the

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:36.880
town was never turned off.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.430
The owner also paid for the entire town to
be cleaned and maintained every year to maintain

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.989
its appeal to potential buyers.

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:51.549
So for over 30 years Kitsault has stood, frozen
in time, life looks the very same as the day

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:55.709
it was evacuated, electricity and all.

00:02:55.709 --> 00:02:58.860
There's just one thing missing, the people.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:05.299
But the owner's plan worked, because in 2004,
the entire town was sold to an Indian-American

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:09.099
businessman for $5.7 million.

00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:14.010
He initially claimed he was going to refurbish
the entire town and turn it into a paradise

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:19.250
community for highly intellectual individuals,
where they could collaborate on inventive

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.499
knew projects and push the boundaries of science,
amongst the stunning scenery.

00:03:24.499 --> 00:03:28.849
The entire town would be refurbished out of
his own pocket and he wouldn't seek to make

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:30.749
any profit from it.

00:03:30.749 --> 00:03:36.689
But unfortunately that turned out to be a
complete lie, it was recently announced that

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:42.030
Kitsault is being turned into a Liquified
Natural Gas plant to export highly valuable

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:45.389
and profitable natural gas.

00:03:45.389 --> 00:03:49.450
The whole intellectual retreat thing was a
big cover story.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:50.549
So what's the lesson here?

00:03:50.549 --> 00:03:55.529
If a stranger approaches you offering to buy
your abandoned mining town you best be sure

00:03:55.529 --> 00:03:58.019
of their intentions first.

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:03.829
If you are passing through Bogota in Columbia
and dare to venture 20 miles south into the

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:10.280
unforgiving wilderness near the spectacular
Tequendama Falls, you will see an old, imposing

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.029
building precariously perched on a cliff edge.

00:04:14.029 --> 00:04:21.509
Welcome to Hotel Del Salto, built in 1923
by an architect, its goal was to attract wealthy

00:04:21.509 --> 00:04:22.620
tourists.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:28.990
But pollution from the Bogota River took hold
and began to quickly eat away and erode the

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:35.310
foundations of the building, it was quickly
deemed too unsafe to continue hosting guests

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:39.919
and in the early 90s it was completely abandoned.

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:42.150
It has sat there rotting ever since.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.650
But this old ruin has a twisted agenda.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:51.130
During its operational days as a world-class
hotel for the upper echelon of society, the

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:56.250
hotel was the location of many freak suicides.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:05:01.349
There is a local legend that the indigenous
peoples used to jump from Tequendama Falls

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:04.610
to avoid being captured by the Spanish Conquerors.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:09.639
According to the legend the natives would
transform into an eagle halfway down the waterfall

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:12.080
and fly away to safety.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.990
Hotel guests must have taken this legend a
little too seriously because throughout the

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:22.639
years many such guests have thrown themselves
off the cliffs and off the hotel's balconies.

00:05:22.639 --> 00:05:26.530
Sadly for them, they didn't turn into eagles.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:32.479
The hotel was also reported to be haunted
by several tabloid newspapers but it's hardly

00:05:32.479 --> 00:05:35.520
surprising, I mean look at the place.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.030
This melancholy story has a somewhat happy
ending though.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:45.030
In 2011 the National University of Columbia
restored the building to its former glory

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:51.099
and made it into a museum for the public called
Tequendama Falls Museum of Biodiversity and

00:05:51.099 --> 00:05:56.729
Culture, celebrating the national heritage
and environment of Columbia.

00:05:56.729 --> 00:06:04.409
A mine closing down and haunted hotels is
one thing but what could cause an entire town

00:06:04.409 --> 00:06:10.810
of 1,500 people in India to disappear in the
middle of the night?

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:16.690
Deep within the Great Indian Desert lies a
once thriving community called Kuldhara.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:23.099
This medium-sized village has driven historians
and the Indian community crazy for over 200

00:06:23.099 --> 00:06:29.110
years, because these mysterious ruins hold
a dark secret that so far, nobody has been

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:30.870
able to explain.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:36.190
Kuldhara was first settled in the 13th century
by the pioneering Paliwal people from the

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.319
nearby city of Pali.

00:06:38.319 --> 00:06:44.830
The population grew and by the 19th century
there were over 1,500 residents in Kuldhara,

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:48.039
mostly farmers, bankers and agricultural traders.

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:54.569
The Paliwal people were known for being business-minded
and the village was prosperous.

00:06:54.569 --> 00:07:01.300
Until one fateful night in 1825 the village
was suddenly deserted.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:05.840
In the middle of the night the entirety of
the settlement's residents abandoned their

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.050
homes and disappeared into the darkness.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:14.669
Sure, many settlements have been abandoned
throughout history due to plagues, famine,

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:20.870
drought and natural disasters but most are
abandoned slowly over time.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:27.729
What's so eerie about Kuldhara is that every
single resident left all together at the same

00:07:27.729 --> 00:07:34.330
time, and furthermore, in the dead of the
night, as if they were hiding from someone

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:36.810
or something.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:43.759
To add more fuel to this bizarre fire, there
were no reports of 1,500+ people looking for

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:50.780
refuge in nearby towns and cities the following
day, or the following week or the following

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.520
month for that matter.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.879
Nobody actually saw them leave the village
and nobody has found any trace of where they

00:07:58.879 --> 00:08:00.099
went.

00:08:00.099 --> 00:08:06.139
They simply disappeared, never to be seen
again.

00:08:06.139 --> 00:08:12.570
To this day nobody knows what happened that
night at Kuldhara to cause the disappearance.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:19.250
But local legends claim that before they left
the Paliwals imposed a curse on the village

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:26.819
that anyone who stepped foot in it would experience
paranormal activity and death, so that nobody

00:08:26.819 --> 00:08:29.430
could ever re-occupy it again.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:33.960
And thus the village has gained a reputation
in Rajasthan for being haunted.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:41.640
In 2010 Gaurav Tiwari, a paranormal investigator
from the Indian Paranormal Society along with

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.130
his team of 12 other people spent a night
in the abandoned village.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:53.290
They claimed to have heard creepy voices and
screams and seen talking spirits.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:09:00.100
Incidentally Tiwari died under mysterious
circumstances at his home only a few years

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.860
after visiting Kuldhara.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:08.230
Some have said this village was deserted in
the dead of night due to some evil paranormal

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:16.320
force whilst some historians claim a powerful
local tyrant called Salim Singh fell in love

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.840
with the daughter of the village's chief.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:24.380
Singh gave the villagers a deadline of two
days for the chief's daughter to marry him

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:30.630
or he would massively raise taxes on the village's
residents that would economically cripple

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:31.740
to community.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:37.580
And so they fled in the middle of the night
out of fear and to avoid further persecution

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:40.090
by the unforgiving Singh.

00:09:40.090 --> 00:10:01.510
There are many such theories but none have
been proven.

